Kokkos kernel and copy census
=============================

A Kokkos Tools library which counts and times every kernel launch by
name and every host-device copy by the labels of the two views, and a
script which turns two runs of different length into per-step numbers.
It needs no change to SPARTA: any KOKKOS build with Kokkos_ENABLE_LIBDL
(the default) loads it through the KOKKOS_TOOLS_LIBS variable.

Build the tool once:

    g++ -shared -fPIC -O2 -o kp_count.so kp_count.cpp

Run the input for N and for 2N steps with the tool loaded:

    KOKKOS_TOOLS_LIBS=/path/kp_count.so KP_COUNT_OUT=r40.txt \
        spa_kokkos_cuda -k on g 1 -sf kk -in in.bench40
    KOKKOS_TOOLS_LIBS=/path/kp_count.so KP_COUNT_OUT=r80.txt \
        spa_kokkos_cuda -k on g 1 -sf kk -in in.bench80

    python3 perstep.py r40.txt r80.txt 40

The difference of the two runs divided by the step difference is the
per-step count, which cancels setup and post-run.  Under MPI every rank
writes its own report, KP_COUNT_OUT with the rank appended.

Environment variables the tool reads:

    KP_COUNT_OUT   report file (stderr when unset)
    KP_COUNT_BT    a substring of a view label: every copy of a view whose
                   label contains it is also keyed by the SPARTA routines on
                   its call stack, so the copies of one array (for example
                   particle:particles) are attributed to their callers.
                   Needs a build with symbols (-g or -rdynamic).

Report format: one line per kernel, "K count seconds name", and one per
copy, "C count MB dst <- src".  A kernel's name is the label given to its
parallel_for/scan/reduce, or the type of its functor or lambda when it has
none.  The seconds are host wall time between the begin and end callbacks:
on a GPU that is launch time for asynchronous kernels unless the run
fences after them, so use the counts and the copy volumes on a GPU, and the
times on a CPU backend.
